/-!
# A verified checker for decompilation

An agent proposes a decomposition of an x86-64 code region into functions;
`Decompile.check` verifies it. The proposal is untrusted data, the checker is
decidable and total, and every rejection names the address or range that caused
it so that the proposal can be repaired.

* `Decompile.Region`: code regions with a base address, addressed decoding, and
  the bridge from a static listing to `Ext.fetch`.
* `Decompile.Edges`: the static control-flow successors of one instruction, and
  the proof that `Ext.exec` produces no others.
* `Decompile.Functions`: proposals, the checker, and its soundness — one
  retirement from inside an accepted function stays inside it, enters a
  function entry, returns, or sits at a declared relocated call site.
* `Decompile.Cfg`: proposed control-flow graphs for one function, the checker
  that verifies one against an accepted partition, and its soundness — inside a
  block control walks forward, and a block is left only along a listed edge.
* `Decompile.Lift`: proposed Lir blocks for recovered blocks, the checker that
  re-emits a proposed block with the backend's own instruction selection and
  compares it with the recovered instructions, and its soundness — the recovered
  bytes are the compiled bytes, so the backend's correctness theory applies to
  them. `Lift.checkFunc_behaviour` composes that with `Machine.Lift` into a
  statement about a lifted program's behaviour.
* `Decompile.SemLift`: the semantic sibling of `Lift`, for code our backend
  did not emit. A proposal is a block summary — live-out registers, stores and
  flags as expressions over the entry state — and the checker decodes the bytes
  and symbolically executes them over those expressions. `check_sound` turns
  acceptance into a run of the real machine.
* `Decompile.Propose`: untrusted heuristics, from an ELF symbol table or from
  stripped code.
* `Decompile.CfgPropose`: the untrusted leader algorithm that proposes a
  control-flow graph for one function.
* `Decompile.Tactic`: `split_functions`, `recover_cfg` and `decode_region`,
  which close a `check`, `checkCfg` or `Region.decode?` goal by kernel
  reduction. Compiled evaluation is not used.
* `Decompile.LiftTactic`: `lift_block`, the same for `Lift.checkBlock` and
  `Lift.checkFunc`.
* `Decompile.Examples`: `MD5_memset` checked by the kernel — its partition, and
  the four-block control-flow graph of its loop — and the whole `.text` of the
  ingested `clang -O0` `sha256.o` checked by evaluation.

The user-facing account of the toolkit is `docs/Decompilation.md`, with an
agent playbook per rung in `docs/agents/`.
-/
